WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Villagers hug trees in Uttarakhand to stop commercial logging and protect their watersheds.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.020
Only to see resin tapping quotas rise on the remaining pines and accelerate the very

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.400
forest loss they had fought.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.660
This is Unintended Consequences, Episode 108.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:23.220
In the steep valleys of Uttarakhand during the 1970s, groups of villagers linked arms around

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.940
the trunks of cheer pine trees, while contractors waited with saws and axes.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.340
The action was intended to halt large-scale commercial felling that was stripping slopes

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.780
and threatening the stream's villagers relied on for irrigation and drinking water.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.920
Instead, the resulting restrictions on timber removal prompted forest authorities to increase

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.440
resin tapping on the surviving stands, leaving many of those same trees weakened and more

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.300
likely to die from insect attack.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.720
The chip co-actions grew from long-standing local concern over the condition of village

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.080
forests and the water they supplied.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.240
People living on the slopes had watched commercial timber operations remove large numbers

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.500
of trees, and they feared the loss of soil stability and reliable spring flow that followed.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.020
At the time, the most direct way to address that pressure appeared to be stopping the logging

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.180
contracts outright.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.240
The villagers who organized the tree-hugging protests were acting on the information

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.220
available to them.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.060
Visible erosion reduced water in channels, and the clear link between heavy cutting and

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:28.700
those changes.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.720
Their goal was straightforward protection of the watershed functions that supported daily

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.560
life in the hills.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.000
Rational observers could see the same pattern of degradation, and could understand why

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.840
a visible, physical barrier to logging seemed like the appropriate response.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.920
One objection that arises here is whether the protesters simply overlooked other economic

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:50.940
uses of the forest.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.400
The record shows they focused on the most obvious and immediate threat.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.040
Full removal of canopy trees, because that removal produced the fastest visible change

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.160
in slope stability and stream volume.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.660
The arithmetic of daily life made the choice rational.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:12.920
Each felled tree represented both lost future water and lost soil that could no longer anchor

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:13.920
the next monsoon.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:19.700
By concentrating on that single, measurable harm, the movement aligned its tactics with

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.540
the clearest causal chain people could observe on the ground.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:28.300
Once the protests succeeded in blocking new logging permits in key areas, state forest

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.300
departments adjusted their management plans to maintain revenue and employment.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:38.020
With timber extraction curtailed, resin collection from living pine trees became the primary

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.020
remaining commercial activity on the protected stands.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:45.840
Quotas for tapping were raised on the trees that remained after the bans took effect.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.920
Early reports noted that the movement had achieved its immediate aim of keeping axes

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.080
out of certain forests.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.260
Forest officials described the shift to resin as a sustainable alternative that would not

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:58.220
require felling.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.580
Local communities continued to monitor the slopes, satisfied that the most destructive

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.400
form of extraction had been checked.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.500
A natural question is whether the departments could have simply reduced overall extraction

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.760
targets rather than shifting the method.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.400
The practical constraint was that forest departments operated under revenue expectations set

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.540
by state budgets, so the same land base still needed to generate income.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.640
Resentapping required only periodic visits by workers with simple tools rather than heavy

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.600
equipment or road building, which made it appear compatible with the new conservation rules.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.120
The policy therefore treated the living tree as a renewable tap rather than a one-time

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.180
timber source, without yet examining how repeated wounding would interact with the tree's

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.180
own defenses over successive seasons.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.620
The expanded resin tapping program placed repeated cuts into the bark of the same pine

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:52.920
trees year after year.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.620
Each tapping wound removed protective layers and created entry points for insects and disease.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.200
Over time, the cumulative stress made the trees far more vulnerable to bark beetle outbreaks

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.600
that spread through the stands.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.280
What had begun as an effort to preserve watershed cover instead produced a gradual but

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.260
accelerating loss of the very trees the movement had shielded from saws.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.940
The causal chain ran through the changed incentive structure.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.540
Once logging was restricted, the forest department needed another source of income from the same

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.300
land, and resin offered an immediate option that did not require removing trunks.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.420
Because the tapping was spread across surviving trees rather than concentrated on a smaller

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.660
area, the damage was distributed but widespread.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:43.280
Second order effects appeared when weakened trees began to fall during storms, further

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.460
disturbing soil on slopes already sensitive to erosion.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.700
Third order effects included reduced canopy cover that altered local microclimates and affected

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:57.280
understory plants and wildlife that had depended on the original forest structure.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.680
Villagers who had hugged the trees to keep their water sources intact found themselves

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.280
facing new patches of dead and dying pine where the tapping had been most intensive.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.760
One might ask whether the beetles would have appeared anyway.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.460
The difference lies in the lowered resistance threshold created by the additional wounds,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.540
which allowed beetle populations to cross from endemic to epidemic levels more readily

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.600
than they would have in untapped stands.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.800
The same trees that had been protected from the saw now carried dozens of resin channels

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.700
per hectare, each channel acting as a potential infection court.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.660
Over multiple years the cumulative effect compounded because each new tapping season re-opened

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.240
or extended existing wounds rather than allowing full closure.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.200
The result was not an immediate collapse but a slow shift in forest composition that only

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.800
became measurable after the policy had been in place long enough for the stress to accumulate

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:53.300
across entire catchments.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.420
As the pattern of beetle-related mortality became visible, forest managers began to reconsider

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.240
the intensity of resin quotas on stressed stands.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.800
Some areas saw reduced tapping schedules or temporary closures while trees recovered

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.720
or were replaced.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:14.060
In other locations, the practice continued at lower levels under revised guidelines.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.400
Instead, management shifted toward more selective and monitored extraction.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.840
Later conservation efforts in the region incorporated lessons about the cumulative effects of non-lethal

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.060
but repeated harvesting.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:33.280
Today, the same hills remain under active community and state oversight, with resin collection

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:38.020
still present but generally operating under tighter limits than during the peak expansion

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:38.620
period.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.240
Incentive structures tend to redirect pressure rather than remove it, so any restriction

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:49.260
on one form of resource use should be examined for the substitute activities it will encourage.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:55.500
Complex ecological systems respond to changes in extraction patterns with delays that can

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:00.460
mask the connection between policy and outcome until damage is already widespread.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:05.560
When a single visible threat is blocked, it is worth mapping the full set of remaining

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:10.540
economic demands on the same resource to anticipate where stress will next appear.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.460
The question for anyone designing conservation rules today is how the next most profitable

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.340
use will behave once the first option is limited.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:21.180
We'll leave it there for today.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.460
Thanks for thinking through this one with me.

